# Operations and observability

Permanent rule for making Fajita's important behavior observable, recoverable, and safe to release. Complements `security-and-privacy.mdc` (redaction), `perceived-performance.mdc` (user-facing async behavior), and `state-completeness.mdc` (error and loading states).

> An error that can be observed, explained, and recovered from is an operational event. An invisible error is product corruption.

> A premium SaaS experience is defined as much by what happens when something goes wrong as by what happens when everything works.

## What must be diagnosable

Application errors, API failures, authentication failures, billing webhooks, background jobs, scheduled jobs, email delivery, imports, exports, AI operations, integrations, uploads, database failures, rate limits, third-party outages, performance regressions, and administrative actions.

## Structured logging

Emit structured logs with enough context to investigate problems, without logging sensitive content. Where appropriate, capture: timestamp, environment, service, operation, request or trace identifier, user identifier in a privacy-conscious form (opaque ID, never email or name), workspace identifier, job identifier, provider, error category, retry count, duration, and outcome.

Never log: passwords, full access tokens, secret keys, private signing secrets, full payment details, sensitive uploaded content, raw private prompts (without an explicit documented safe policy), or unnecessary personal information.

## Required operational capabilities

- Error monitoring.
- Performance monitoring where appropriate.
- Health checks.
- Background-job visibility.
- Retry limits.
- Exponential backoff where appropriate.
- Dead-letter or failed-job handling.
- Safe manual recovery.
- Idempotency for anything that can be retried.
- Alert thresholds with defined ownership.
- Environment separation (dev, preview, production never share state or keys).
- Feature flags for risky rollouts.
- Rollback planning.
- Backup awareness.
- Status-page accuracy.
- Incident documentation.

## Support without exposure

Observability must support customer support and diagnostics without exposing internal or sensitive data to customers. Support-facing views show enough to help; they never reveal secrets, other tenants' data, stack traces, or internal infrastructure.

## Repository-specific baseline (known facts)

- No error-monitoring or performance-monitoring vendor is wired yet. Selection and thresholds are `[UNRESOLVED]`.
- The Stripe webhook route returns 400 on failure and relies on Stripe's own retry behavior; app-side retry, dead-letter, and idempotency are not yet implemented.
- DataFast tracks pageviews, goals, and AI-crawler traffic (`src/middleware.ts`); it is product analytics, not operational error monitoring. Do not conflate the two.
- No background-job runner or queue is configured yet. Monitoring checks, alert delivery, and scheduled tasks implied by the product are `[UNRESOLVED]` operations to design before implementation.
- Hosting: Vercel (`.vercel` present). Environment separation should use provider environment scoping.

## Required memory updates

- `.cursor/maturity-memory/observability-plan.md`
- `.cursor/maturity-memory/background-job-register.md`
- `.cursor/maturity-memory/incident-playbook.md`

Before implementing or releasing background jobs, scheduled tasks, webhooks, email delivery, AI generation, imports, exports, third-party integrations, data pipelines, long-running tasks, or high-value customer actions, invoke `operations-and-observability-architect`.

## Avoid overengineering

Prefer provider-native logging, managed cron, and a single well-chosen error monitor over a custom observability stack. Do not add tracing infrastructure, extra vendors, or metrics no one will read. Track the small set of signals that map to real alerts and real recovery actions.

